Agree. The "you can just match perf with inline asm" argument is funny: it implies that languages don't matter at all: but obviously the world can't afford to rewrite all software for all chips/variants. Even more true with the shift to accelerators which Mojo🔥 is built for. 🚀
Quoting @IsmiLazimDel87
@oliora @NickSmit_ @tinrab @clattner_llvm You indeed can. But that just leads to endless rabbit hole that makes things messier and messier. You become compiler and platform dependent in doing so. Your code becomes far uglier and verbose.

So it begins :), Endia is an exciting community project building on MAX and Mojo:
import endia as nd
def foo(x: List[nd.Array]) -> nd.Array:
return nd.sum((x[0] * x[0] + x[1] * x[1]))
x = nd.Array("[[1.0,2.0,3.0],[1.0,2.0,3.0]]", requires_grad=True)
y = nd.Array("[[1.0,2.0,3.0],[1.0,2.0,3.0]]", requires_grad=True)
grads = nd.grad(outs=foo(List(x, y)), inputs=List(x, y), retain_graph=True)[
List[nd.Array]
]
print(grads[0], grads[1])

